# [MUST] Codebase exploration

- **codebase-memory-mcp is the primary structural navigation tool.** Inspect the
  existing architecture and patterns BEFORE introducing new modules, services,
  ports, repositories, controllers or events.
- Tool ladder, in order:
  1. `codebase-memory-mcp` — symbols and definitions, callers/callees, dependency
     analysis, impact analysis, module boundaries, existing implementation patterns.
  2. `Read` — only AFTER the graph has identified the relevant file/symbol.
  3. `grep` / `Glob` — exact textual searches, or when the graph cannot reliably
     answer the question.
- Never invent modules, files, classes, routes or architectural patterns. If the
  graph does not show it, verify in source before asserting that it exists.
- Coverage is best-effort, never proof of completeness: `check_index_coverage`
  every cited path, and re-verify with `grep` before any negative or exhaustive
  claim ("there is no X", "nothing calls Y").

## Known graph blind spots (verified 2026-08-24)

- **Routes.** Laravel route definitions are runtime closures the indexer cannot
  resolve — the graph holds 2 `Route` nodes for the whole HTTP surface. For any
  route question, read the route files directly: `routes/web.php`,
  `routes/settings.php`, `routes/api.php`, `routes/console.php`, plus the module
  file mounted by `AuthServiceProvider::registerWebRoutes()` →
  `src/Modules/Auth/Infrastructure/Routes/web.php`.
- **Blade.** Templates under `resources/views/` are indexed as opaque `Module`
  nodes and 14 files are flagged `parse_partial`. A Blade view is NOT evidence of
  an implemented backend feature — confirm the controller / model / migration
  exists before treating it as one. Live example:
  `resources/views/emails/appointments/*` and
  `resources/views/exports/pdf/appointments.blade.php` have zero backing PHP.

## Read budget — the other half of the ladder

The ladder above says WHICH tool to reach for. This says HOW MUCH to pull. The
cost that blows up a thread is payload, not call count: one `cat` of a 250-line
provider costs more than six graph queries.

- **Never dump a whole file to read one symbol.** Once the graph has the
  qualified name, use `get_code_snippet` — it returns that symbol, not its 400
  neighbours. `cat`/`Read` of a full file is the fallback, not the default.
- **Intent without bodies.** `search_graph(fields: ["docstring", "signature"])`
  returns the docblock and signature of every match in one call. Most "why is
  this built this way" questions are answered there — reach for it BEFORE
  opening files, because this project documents its reasoning in docblocks.
- **Full-file reads are justified only when the file IS the answer**, and are
  capped at ~6 per exploratory task:
  - composition roots / service providers (`*ServiceProvider.php`)
  - route files (the graph cannot resolve them — see blind spots above)
  - `config/*.php`
  - files flagged `parse_partial` by `check_index_coverage`
  Anything else: snippet first, escalate to the full file only if the snippet
  proved insufficient.
- **A `for f in dir/*.php; do cat "$f"; done` loop is ONE call and N files of
  payload.** Batching calls is good; batching payload is the problem this rule
  exists to stop. Outline first (`grep -nE "class |public function"`), then pull
  only the bodies that matter.
- **Self-check before answering an "analyze / explain / how does X work"
  question:** if full-file reads outnumber graph queries by more than ~2:1, the
  ladder was skipped. Say so and prefer the graph next time.

> Under Claude Code's auto mode, `Read`/`Grep`/`Glob` are executed as `cat` /
> `grep` / `find` inside Bash. A transcript showing "Read × 0, Bash × 20" is NOT
> evidence the ladder was followed — count what the Bash calls actually did.

